1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,235
Root can do anything. So you never hand out the root password.

2
00:00:04,255 --> 00:00:08,961
Instead, you give each person exactly the power they need, and nothing more.

3
00:00:08,981 --> 00:00:12,753
That is privileged access, and on Linux it is done with sudo.

4
00:00:13,223 --> 00:00:15,806
In this video: configure privileged access.

5
00:00:20,326 --> 00:00:22,909
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:23,379 --> 00:00:25,023
Configure privileged access.

7
00:00:25,493 --> 00:00:29,625
We'll see who can use sudo, give a user full sudo with the wheel group,

8
00:00:29,645 --> 00:00:32,491
write a rule for one single command, and test it.

9
00:00:40,011 --> 00:00:43,228
Sudo reads its rules from the file /etc/sudoers,

10
00:00:43,248 --> 00:00:48,152
and from small files in the folder /etc/sudoers.d. A rule has parts. Who:

11
00:00:48,172 --> 00:00:51,996
a user, or a percent sign (%) and a group. On which host:

12
00:00:52,016 --> 00:00:56,515
all. As which user, in parentheses: all. Options, like no password.

13
00:00:56,535 --> 00:00:59,212
And last, the commands that are allowed.

14
00:01:09,682 --> 00:01:15,068
Never edit the sudoers file with a normal editor: one typo can lock everyone out.

15
00:01:15,088 --> 00:01:17,471
Use visudo, which checks the syntax.

16
00:01:17,491 --> 00:01:21,875
In this video the lab writes the file with tee, so we can show it,

17
00:01:21,895 --> 00:01:27,348
and then checks it with visudo -c. In this demo, the lab changes nothing silently.

18
00:01:36,868 --> 00:01:40,878
First, who can use sudo? sudo -l lists what the current user may run.

19
00:01:40,898 --> 00:01:44,383
Our lab user student may run everything, without a password.

20
00:01:44,403 --> 00:01:46,661
That is how the lab image was prepared,

21
00:01:46,681 --> 00:01:50,166
so on your own system sudo will probably ask for a password.

22
00:01:50,186 --> 00:01:54,547
The long list of default settings is not shown here. Next, the group wheel:

23
00:01:54,567 --> 00:01:58,578
it is empty. And in the sudoers file, one rule: %wheel ALL=(ALL) ALL.

24
00:01:58,598 --> 00:02:01,381
Everyone in the group wheel may run any command.

25
00:02:14,901 --> 00:02:19,705
So, to give a user full sudo, put the user in the group wheel.

26
00:02:19,725 --> 00:02:25,619
We create alice, then usermod -aG wheel alice. The group line now has alice.

27
00:02:25,639 --> 00:02:29,665
sudo -l -U alice shows what alice may run: all, all.

28
00:02:41,185 --> 00:02:43,973
Now a smaller power. We create bob.

29
00:02:43,993 --> 00:02:48,226
Then one rule, written into a file in /etc/sudoers.d:

30
00:02:48,246 --> 00:02:52,720
bob may run only /usr/bin/systemctl, without a password.

31
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:57,855
The echo command prints the rule, and tee writes it to the file.

32
00:02:57,875 --> 00:03:02,349
Sudo files must be readable only: mode 0440, with chmod.

33
00:03:13,869 --> 00:03:18,909
Now check. visudo -c checks the syntax of all sudo files.

34
00:03:18,929 --> 00:03:22,016
Parsed OK, for each, including bob.

35
00:03:22,036 --> 00:03:26,899
The file 90-cloud-init-users is part of this lab image.

36
00:03:26,919 --> 00:03:33,113
sudo -l -U bob lists his rights: only /usr/bin/systemctl, no password.

37
00:03:42,633 --> 00:03:48,495
And a real test. sudo -u bob runs a command as bob. Inside it, sudo -n means:

38
00:03:48,515 --> 00:03:53,689
never ask for a password. First, systemctl is-active sshd. It works:

39
00:03:53,709 --> 00:03:58,884
the answer is active. Then the command id, which is not in the rule:

40
00:03:58,904 --> 00:04:04,842
sudo says a password is required. So bob can do his one job, and nothing else.

41
00:04:14,362 --> 00:04:17,798
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

42
00:04:19,318 --> 00:04:23,651
We reboot. Alice is still in the group wheel, and bob still has his rule.

43
00:04:23,671 --> 00:04:26,573
The rules are in files, so they survive a reboot.

44
00:04:34,093 --> 00:04:39,171
Your turn. The lab silently creates the users gina and hank first.

45
00:04:39,191 --> 00:04:41,333
Put gina in the group wheel.

46
00:04:41,353 --> 00:04:46,354
Give hank the right to run /usr/bin/systemctl without a password,

47
00:04:46,374 --> 00:04:50,061
in the file /etc/sudoers.d/hank, with mode 0440.

48
00:04:50,081 --> 00:04:53,614
Run the checker, then reboot and run it again.

49
00:05:04,084 --> 00:05:05,835
Try before you open the solution.

50
00:05:11,355 --> 00:05:17,031
Next up: configure firewall settings using firewall-cmd and firewalld.

51
00:05:17,501 --> 00:05:20,404
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
